    "cover_story": "<p style='width:1000px;'>We recently videotaped a public park where nearby children go. Our goal is to understand children's behavior in a familiar park setting.<br><br>To protect their privacy, we used an algorithm that represents each child as a dot and converts their  trajectories in the park into movement of the dots.<br><br><b>Throughout the experiment, there will be two dots in each video, representing two children in the park.</b></p>",

    "instructions_txt_b": '<p style="width:1000px;"> This experiment has <b>6 sections</b>. <br><br><u>In 3 sections (situation 1)</u>, one dot may be <strong>chasing</strong> the other or the dots may be <strong>moving independently</strong>.<br><br><u>In the other 3 sections (situation 2)</u>, the dots may be engaged in either a <strong> positive interaction (e.g., playing) </strong> or a<br><strong>negative interaction (e.g., fighting)</strong>.<br><br>After each video, you will be asked 1-2 questions.</p>',

    "task_intro": '<p style="width:1000px;">Every few videos, your task will switch between the two situations, so <strong>pay attention to the instructions!</strong><br><br>Demos of each situation are on the next few pages.<br><br>Except during breaks, keep your eyes on the screen.<br><br><b>If you miss more than 10% of responses, you will not be compensated</strong>.</b></p>',

